# RLS Policies: Performance Patterns

## Pyramid Layer

- Layer: L3 SQL leaf.

## Use This When

- Load this after the RLS router when the policy is functionally correct but needs better execution characteristics.

## Stop Here If

- Stop once the policy performance fix is clear.

## Descend To

- Return to `/.cursor/rules/create-rls-policies.mdc` for authoring or helper-function siblings.

## Add Indexes

Add indexes for columns referenced by policy predicates.

```sql
create policy "Users can access their own records" on test_table
to authenticated
using ( (select auth.uid()) = user_id );

create index userid
on test_table
using btree (user_id);
```

## Wrap Stable Helpers in `select`

```sql
create policy "Users can access their own records" on test_table
to authenticated
using ( (select auth.uid()) = user_id );
```

This lets Postgres evaluate stable helper results per statement instead of per row.

## Minimize Joins

Prefer set-based membership checks over row-by-row joins to the source table.

```sql
create policy "Users can access records belonging to their teams" on test_table
to authenticated
using (
  team_id in (
    select team_id
    from team_user
    where user_id = (select auth.uid())
  )
);
```

## Specify Roles Explicitly

Always use `to authenticated` or `to anon, authenticated` as needed so the predicate does not execute for irrelevant roles.